# RegrooveOS systemd presets.
#
# Preset files are processed in filename order and the first line matching a
# unit decides it, so 50- is read before Debian's 90-systemd.preset. That file
# says nothing about console-getty.service, so the line below is what decides
# it. (If it ever did, this file would still win, and this comment would be
# the thing to check.)
#
# WHY A PRESET AND NOT A WANTS SYMLINK IN mkosi.extra
#
# Putting getty.target.wants/console-getty.service in mkosi.extra does not
# work, and fails silently. That symlink has to point at
# /usr/lib/systemd/system/console-getty.service, which is provided by the
# systemd package and does not exist inside mkosi.extra -- so within the
# source tree the link dangles, and mkosi does not carry it across. Measured
# on a built dev image: getty.target.wants contained only getty@tty1.service,
# the one Debian enables, and console-getty.service was simply absent. The
# contrast is local-fs.target.wants/boot-firmware.mount, which resolves to
# ../boot-firmware.mount inside the tree and does survive.
#
# A preset is a plain file, so it copies. mkosi applies it by running
# `systemctl --root=/buildroot preset-all` (run_preset, mkosi/__init__.py)
# after the extra trees are installed, which is also how mkosi itself enables
# ssh -- see the 80-mkosi-ssh.preset it writes.
#
# WHY console-getty.service AT ALL
#
# It runs agetty on /dev/console, and /dev/console is whichever tty the kernel
# made the preferred console: the last console= on the command line that
# registered successfully. On a Pi 4 that is ttyAMA1. Naming a tty directly
# (serial-getty@ttyAMA1.service) would hard-code one board, and the numbering
# is not guaranteed across Pi 5 / CM5; this follows the board instead, which
# is the same reason mkosi.conf keeps one config.txt for all four boards.
#
# This is needed because systemd's getty generator did not create a serial
# getty on its own, even though systemd-getty-generator(8) states that
# redirecting the kernel console with console= "is sufficient to ... get both
# kernel messages and a getty prompt on a serial TTY". With
# console=tty1 console=ttyAMA0 console=ttyAMA1 on cmdline.txt and a working
# PL011, the generator produced getty@tty1 and nothing else, so the box booted
# to a serial console that printed everything and accepted no login.
#
# The equivalent Autologin= patch mkosi applies to this unit (dev profile
# only) is what turns it into a root shell instead of a login prompt.
enable console-getty.service
